% Who calls whom in an ML program, and what is never called
% (docs/plans/plan_prolog.md, stage 10), over the facts of mini-ml
% -facts (Closure_facts.mli) of all the program's units and the answers
% of pointer.dl, run with this file: call_edge(I, F), the call I may
% reach the function F.
%   calls(F, G)        in F's body, a call that may reach G
%   never_called(F)    a function written that no call reaches
%   unreached(F)       one that no call reaches from the units' toplevels:
%                      never called, or called only from what is not
%   half_called(F)     given its first argument somewhere, its next nowhere
%   unresolved(I)      a call that reaches nothing: of a value the facts
%                      do not follow, or in code that never runs

called(F) :- call_edge(I, F).
calls(F, G) :- in_function(I, F), call_edge(I, G).
is_inner(F) :- inner(F, G).
never_called(F) :- closure(F, U, L), \+ called(F), \+ is_inner(F).
half_called(G) :- inner(F, G), called(G), \+ called(F).
resolved(I) :- call_edge(I, F).
unresolved(I) :- call_indirect(I, V), \+ resolved(I).

reached(F) :- init(F).
reached(G) :- reached(F), calls(F, G).
unreached(F) :- closure(F, U, L), \+ reached(F), \+ is_inner(F).
% a call in code that runs and that reaches nothing: a hole in the facts
% (a value they do not follow), each to be looked at
%
% cs-history:
% Olin Shivers's control-flow analysis (PLDI 1988, his thesis 1991):
% which lambdas a call of Scheme may reach, k-CFA telling apart the
% last k call sites (the contexts); 0-CFA, the one here, none. k-CFA
% for k >= 1 was shown EXPTIME-complete for functional programs
% (David Van Horn, Harry Mairson, 2008), where for objects the same
% idea is polynomial: Matthew Might, Yannis Smaragdakis and Van Horn
% explained the paradox (PLDI 2010): a closure keeps its whole
% environment, an object only its fields.
%
% others:
% Doop (Martin Bravenboer, Yannis Smaragdakis, OOPSLA 2009): Java's
% pointer analysis and its call graph as Datalog alone, each analysis
% of the literature a few hundred lines of rules, faster than the hand
% written ones (Paddle's); its contexts chosen by a rule: a call site's
% (k-CFA), the receiver object's (object sensitivity: Ana Milanova,
% Atanas Rountev, Barbara Ryder, 2002), its type's (type sensitivity:
% Smaragdakis, Bravenboer, Ondrej Lhotak, POPL 2011, "Pick your
% contexts well"). Context sensitivity is one more column: a variable
% points to an object in a context, and a call's rule says which
% context the callee gets, so the rules here would take it as they
% are, the relations wider. Smaragdakis and George Balatsouras's
% "Pointer Analysis" (Foundations and Trends in Programming Languages,
% 2015) is the field's survey, in those rules.
%
% References: Olin Shivers, "Control-flow analysis of higher-order
% languages" (CMU thesis, 1991); Martin Bravenboer, Yannis Smaragdakis,
% "Strictly declarative specification of sophisticated points-to
% analyses" (OOPSLA 2009); Matthew Might, Yannis Smaragdakis, David Van
% Horn, "Resolving and exploiting the k-CFA paradox" (PLDI 2010);
% Yannis Smaragdakis, George Balatsouras, "Pointer Analysis" (2015).
hole(I, F) :- unresolved(I), in_function(I, F), reached(F).
